WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.060
<v SPEAKER_00>You have been building a transcription workflow for research recordings.</v>

2
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:06.940
<v SPEAKER_00>Thank you for joining us today, Dr Rossi.</v>

3
00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:09.460
<v SPEAKER_00>What problem were you trying to solve?</v>

4
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:15.920
<v SPEAKER_01>Research interviews are full of personal information: names, places and details that could identify a participant.</v>

5
00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:22.760
<v SPEAKER_01>Uploading them to a cloud service moves that information outside the institution, which many ethics approvals do not allow.</v>

6
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:29.600
<v SPEAKER_01>This workflow runs every step on hardware the institution controls, whether that is a laptop or a university cluster.</v>

7
00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:32.460
<v SPEAKER_00>Which models does it rely on?</v>

8
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:40.720
<v SPEAKER_01>Speech is transcribed by Whisper through the Hugging Face transformers library, and speakers are told apart by a pyannote diarisation pipeline.</v>

9
00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:46.120
<v SPEAKER_01>The model weights are downloaded once, and after that a run needs no network connection.</v>

10
00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:50.780
<v SPEAKER_00>What happens to the text once the model has produced it?</v>

11
00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:59.400
<v SPEAKER_01>A series of clean-up stages removes stray characters, repairs spacing and punctuation, and can shorten the runaway repetitions that Whisper occasionally produces.</v>

12
00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:05.160
<v SPEAKER_01>Personal names are then replaced with placeholders, using a curated dictionary of first names and surnames.</v>

13
00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:08.020
<v SPEAKER_00>Does the masking find every name?</v>

14
00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.240
<v SPEAKER_01>No, and it matters to say so.</v>

15
00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:19.520
<v SPEAKER_01>A dictionary finds only the names it contains, and only when they are capitalised, so an unusual or lower-case name can slip through.</v>

16
00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:25.280
<v SPEAKER_01>Every masked transcript therefore recommends a review, and the paper reports how often names were missed.</v>

17
00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:29.220
<v SPEAKER_00>How far can a researcher trust the speaker labels?</v>

18
00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:31.720
<v SPEAKER_01>That depends on the recording.</v>

19
00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:40.360
<v SPEAKER_01>Overlapping speech, similar voices and distant microphones all make attribution harder, so the labels should be checked against the audio wherever exact attribution matters.</v>

20
00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:44.320
<v SPEAKER_01>The paper reports the error rates we measured on meeting recordings.</v>

21
00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.180
<v SPEAKER_00>What do the timestamped files contain?</v>

22
00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:55.080
<v SPEAKER_01>The JSON file keeps the timing of every segment and every word, and the caption file uses the same timings.</v>

23
00:01:55.120 --> 00:02:05.160
<v SPEAKER_01>Both are written before any clean-up, so they record exactly what the model produced, and with masking switched on a redacted copy of each is written beside them.</v>

24
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:09.120
<v SPEAKER_01>A review file then points to segments worth checking by ear.</v>

25
00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:13.060
<v SPEAKER_00>And how would someone check a transcript months later?</v>

26
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:22.400
<v SPEAKER_01>Each run writes a manifest that lists every file it produced with a digest of its contents, together with the settings and software versions.</v>

27
00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:26.720
<v SPEAKER_01>Checking the files against that manifest shows whether anything has changed since.</v>

28
00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:28.500
<v SPEAKER_00>Thank you, Elena.</v>

29
00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:29.940
<v SPEAKER_00>That was very clear.</v>

